WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.833
Drift detection.

2
00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:04.946
Drift is the reason the lifecycle is a loop.

3
00:00:04.946 --> 00:00:06.800
It degrades a model silently.

4
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.767
Drift comes in two species.

5
00:00:10.067 --> 00:00:14.215
Data drift means the inputs no longer look like the training data.

6
00:00:14.215 --> 00:00:18.300
Concept drift means the same inputs now mean something different.

7
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.118
A recalibrated sensor causes the first.

8
00:00:21.118 --> 00:00:23.700
Evolving fraud tactics cause the second.

9
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.445
Data drift needs no labels to detect.

10
00:00:26.445 --> 00:00:31.400
Concept drift needs ground truth, because inputs may look perfectly normal.

11
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:34.600
Here is data drift for one feature.

12
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.500
The reference window is the training-era data.

13
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.779
The live window has walked away.

14
00:00:39.779 --> 00:00:40.768
Nothing crashed.

15
00:00:40.768 --> 00:00:42.500
That is exactly the problem.

16
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.000
So we compare the two windows with a statistical test, feature by feature.

17
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:49.700
Here is the check in Python.

18
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.267
One window from the training era, one from the current month.

19
00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:57.333
The Kolmogorov Smirnov test compares the two distributions.

20
00:00:57.633 --> 00:01:00.304
If the p value is tiny, we raise an alert.

21
00:01:00.304 --> 00:01:02.467
Run it per feature, on a schedule.

22
00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:05.167
Here is a real example.

23
00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:11.067
After a satellite firmware update, the heater duty cycle feature shifts its whole distribution.

24
00:01:11.367 --> 00:01:13.533
A nightly test flags it within a day.

25
00:01:13.833 --> 00:01:17.967
You investigate, retrain on post-update data, and the loop closes.

26
00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:22.233
A drift alarm is not proof that the model is wrong.

27
00:01:22.533 --> 00:01:27.600
But it is a trigger to investigate, and ignored alarms are how silent failures happen.

28
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:29.585
Let's recap.

29
00:01:29.585 --> 00:01:31.546
Data drift changes the inputs.

30
00:01:31.546 --> 00:01:33.900
Concept drift changes their meaning.

31
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.967
Both degrade a model silently.

32
00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:38.767
Compare a reference window with a live window.

33
00:01:39.067 --> 00:01:41.661
And when an alarm fires, investigate.

34
00:01:41.661 --> 00:01:43.133
Next up: A/B testing.
